- ♪♪ [humming funk tune]

1156
00:50:30,786 --> 00:50:32,788
To destroy George
and his knights.

1157
00:50:32,822 --> 00:50:34,514
Talk about incompetence.

1158
00:50:34,548 --> 00:50:36,067
In three days,
you've only managed

1159
00:50:36,102 --> 00:50:38,380
to destroy three
of these interfering fools

1160
00:50:38,414 --> 00:50:40,106
with all your magic powers.

1161
00:50:40,140 --> 00:50:42,522
- [Tom] One a day.
- Not all my magic powers,
branton.

1162
00:50:42,556 --> 00:50:45,283
- [Joel] Uh, most of them.
- Give me my ring,

1163
00:50:45,318 --> 00:50:48,010
and I'll dispose of these men
in quick order.

1164
00:50:48,045 --> 00:50:49,770
Not till I get
my bicycle back.

1165
00:50:49,805 --> 00:50:52,083
What use is the ring to you?

1166
00:50:52,118 --> 00:50:55,155
No mortal can command its magic.

1167
00:50:55,190 --> 00:50:59,228
You'll get your ring back
when I get the Princess.
That was our bargain.

1168
00:50:59,263 --> 00:51:01,368
- Don't you trust me?
- [Joel mimicking]
"Don't you trust me?"

1169
00:51:01,403 --> 00:51:04,854
- Not an inch.
- You're insolent! What if I
cast a spell over you--

1170
00:51:04,889 --> 00:51:08,444
- no one in my organization
has ever talked to me that way.
- ...And turn you into a dog

1171
00:51:08,479 --> 00:51:10,446
or a rat or a cat
and take the ring?

1172
00:51:10,481 --> 00:51:12,103
- Go ahead.
- [Tom] Make me.

1173
00:51:12,138 --> 00:51:14,381
- Give me my ring, now.
- [Tom] No.

1174
00:51:14,416 --> 00:51:16,832
- Now!
- You gonna cry, crybaby?

1175
00:51:16,866 --> 00:51:19,835
- Cry! Who's gonna cry?
- You can't hurt me
while I wear your ring.

1176
00:51:19,869 --> 00:51:24,322
But once you have it back,
what's to save me
from your curses?

1177
00:51:24,357 --> 00:51:26,773
[Tom]
You're wonderful.

1178
00:51:26,807 --> 00:51:30,880
[Joel] Well, I've prepared
a small demonstration.

1179
00:51:30,915 --> 00:51:32,537
Why would I want to hurt you?

1180
00:51:32,572 --> 00:51:34,056
'Cause you're a jerk.

1181
00:51:34,091 --> 00:51:35,540
Why wouldn't you?

1182
00:51:35,575 --> 00:51:37,473
You're only helping me now
because you want

1183
00:51:37,508 --> 00:51:39,717
to get back this ring
which you so stupidly lost.

1184
00:51:39,751 --> 00:51:41,201
[Joel] Sounds like
a noöel coward play.

1185
00:51:41,236 --> 00:51:42,547
I'll keep my word.

1186
00:51:42,582 --> 00:51:44,204
I'll give it back
in five days' time,

1187
00:51:44,239 --> 00:51:47,828
when these curses are behind me
and I've claimed the Princess.

1188
00:51:47,863 --> 00:51:49,830
[Tom] But, please,
keep the smock as our gift.

1189
00:51:49,865 --> 00:51:53,593
- You're a tough trader,
branton.
- We both know what we want,

1190
00:51:53,627 --> 00:51:57,286
and we're both going to get it
so long as you dispose
of these knights.

1191
00:51:57,321 --> 00:51:58,874
Especially sir George.

1192
00:51:58,908 --> 00:52:00,945
[Joel]
You can negotiate anything,
by basil rathbone.

1193
00:52:00,979 --> 00:52:03,085
George. Yes.

1194
00:52:03,120 --> 00:52:06,088
Twice he's squirmed
out of your trap,
and he could do it again.

1195
00:52:06,123 --> 00:52:08,815
Oh, stop worrying, branton.
You're worse than an old woman.

1196
00:52:08,849 --> 00:52:10,886
- Yeah. He should know.
- If George and his knights

1197
00:52:10,920 --> 00:52:13,060
are not dead by week's end...

1198
00:52:13,095 --> 00:52:16,788
- They'll die long before that--
- hey, look. You can see
the string that works his mouth.

1199
00:52:16,823 --> 00:52:20,482
...and in circumstances
worse than anything
I've conjured up yet.

1200
00:52:22,208 --> 00:52:25,107
One of them's coming here now...

1201
00:52:25,142 --> 00:52:27,385
To his death.

1202
00:52:28,524 --> 00:52:30,457
Stop following me!

1203
00:52:33,702 --> 00:52:36,463
- Well.
- Hello. Hoorah.

1204
00:52:36,498 --> 00:52:38,224
A gallant frenchman.

1205
00:52:38,258 --> 00:52:40,018
[Joel] You don't see
many of those.

1206
00:52:40,053 --> 00:52:42,711
[Tom] ♪ alouette,
gentille alouette ♪

1207
00:52:42,745 --> 00:52:44,368
- ♪ alou--
- [branton mutters]

1208
00:52:44,402 --> 00:52:46,301
- He's on my horse.
- All right.

1209
00:52:46,335 --> 00:52:47,750
And he is a frenchman.

1210
00:52:47,785 --> 00:52:49,718
Don't lose your head.

1211
00:52:50,995 --> 00:52:53,515
Let him come in here.

1212
00:52:53,549 --> 00:52:55,275
We'll be ready for him.

1213
00:52:57,070 --> 00:52:59,831
So, why does the most evil man
in the world
live in a stuckey's?

1214
00:52:59,866 --> 00:53:01,833
[Joel] He likes
the pecan pralines?

1215
00:53:01,868 --> 00:53:04,319
- It's the broasted chicken.
- [Tom] Nut logs.

1216
00:53:05,803 --> 00:53:09,945
♪♪ [Woman singing in French]

1217
00:53:09,979 --> 00:53:14,329
♪♪ [Tom joins in]

1218
00:53:14,363 --> 00:53:15,847
♪♪ [Joel, crow join in]

1219
00:53:15,882 --> 00:53:17,849
[Dennis speaks in French]

1220
00:53:17,884 --> 00:53:19,265
[Responds in French]

1221
00:53:19,299 --> 00:53:20,611
[Dennis speaks in French]

1222
00:53:20,645 --> 00:53:22,820
Mignonette, monsieur.

1223
00:53:22,854 --> 00:53:24,477
[Tom]
What would you do, folks?

1224
00:53:24,511 --> 00:53:26,720
Mignonette.

1225
00:53:28,205 --> 00:53:29,930
[Joel groans]

1226
00:53:29,965 --> 00:53:33,727
Sleeping in armor
is hard on a body.

1227
00:53:36,903 --> 00:53:37,869
Hmm?

1228
00:53:37,904 --> 00:53:40,665
Aw, just let it ring.

1229
00:53:40,700 --> 00:53:42,874
[Tom grunts]

1230
00:53:42,909 --> 00:53:45,291
Huh. Armor with memory.

1231
00:53:50,123 --> 00:53:53,264
- Oh, yeah.
- [Joel]
You taste like Charles boyer.

1232
00:53:53,299 --> 00:53:55,266
So, is this French kissing?

1233
00:53:55,301 --> 00:53:56,647
- [Laughter]
- [Speaks in French]

1234
00:53:56,681 --> 00:54:00,340
- Well...
- Mignonette...
[Continues in French]

1235
00:54:00,375 --> 00:54:03,170
[Poor French accent]
Yes, and those Americans-- they
can't appreciate a good meal.

1236
00:54:03,205 --> 00:54:05,242
[Speaking in French]

1237
00:54:05,276 --> 00:54:07,278
[Joel]
Oh, tish, you spoke French!

1238
00:54:09,970 --> 00:54:11,593
- Uh-uh. Uh-uh.
- [Tom] Come, come.

1239
00:54:11,627 --> 00:54:13,940
We've been dating
for almost two minutes.

1240
00:54:16,391 --> 00:54:18,185
He's pepé le pew.

1241
00:54:20,740 --> 00:54:22,293
- Rowr!
- [Joel] Cat people.

1242
00:54:25,227 --> 00:54:27,816
[Tom] Boy, he really had
the beer goggles on, didn't he?

1243
00:54:27,850 --> 00:54:29,680
- [Joel] Mm-hmm.
- Whoo!

1244
00:54:29,714 --> 00:54:32,717
Hey, all right, everybody!
Frenchy's getting some!

1245
00:54:32,752 --> 00:54:34,340
Whoo!

1246
00:54:34,374 --> 00:54:37,895
[Joel] Oh, man,
I know he's French, but her?

1247
00:54:39,655 --> 00:54:41,001
Spock!

1248
00:54:41,036 --> 00:54:44,039
[Whistles]

1249
00:54:46,731 --> 00:54:48,768
Ah, Barbra Streisand nails.

1250
00:54:53,255 --> 00:54:56,638
[Tom]
What is your secret, man?

1251
00:54:56,672 --> 00:54:59,848
- That was close.
- [Joel] Hey, come on.
She was just a rebound.

1252
00:54:59,882 --> 00:55:02,954
You saved my life, mon ami.

1253
00:55:02,989 --> 00:55:06,199
We frenchmen--
we have a weakness
for a pretty woman.

1254
00:55:06,233 --> 00:55:08,201
- Is that relevant?
- Lordac bewitched you?

1255
00:55:08,235 --> 00:55:11,549
Yes. I should have known
that such a pretty creature

1256
00:55:11,584 --> 00:55:14,172
would not be out
this early hour.

1257
00:55:14,207 --> 00:55:16,589
But I was robbed of all thought.

1258
00:55:16,623 --> 00:55:18,694
- Except one.
- [Crow exclaiming]

1259
00:55:18,729 --> 00:55:21,663
Lordac knows
how we parisians feel.

1260
00:55:21,697 --> 00:55:24,355
- Why did you come here?
- [Tom] Well, I am French,
you know.

1261
00:55:24,390 --> 00:55:26,150
Don't remember?

1262
00:55:26,184 --> 00:55:28,911
Wait.
[Speaks French]

1263
00:55:28,946 --> 00:55:30,396
- What is it?
- Branton!

1264
00:55:30,430 --> 00:55:32,432
- What about him?
- He's in that mill.

1265
00:55:32,467 --> 00:55:35,262
- I'm sure of it.
I saw his horse.
- [Tom exclaims in French]

1266
00:55:35,297 --> 00:55:37,230
- He's still there.
- Did I mention
that I am French?

1267
00:55:39,405 --> 00:55:40,889
[Joel]
Do we need a key?

1268
00:55:40,923 --> 00:55:43,201
- No, just walk right in.
- Hmm.

1269
00:55:43,236 --> 00:55:44,755
[Tom]
Hello. I am French.

1270
00:55:44,789 --> 00:55:47,240
Let's look at the menu
before we order.

1271
00:55:47,274 --> 00:55:48,862
Sir branton!

1272
00:55:48,897 --> 00:55:51,520
Señor branton!

1273
00:55:51,555 --> 00:55:53,522
- Oh.
- Well, good morning, gentlemen.

1274
00:55:53,557 --> 00:55:56,870
[Tom]
Two for smoking, then?

1275
00:55:56,905 --> 00:55:58,941
I'm French, you know.
Very French.

1276
00:55:58,976 --> 00:56:01,323
- Is it good?
- [Joel] Sure, but is it safe?

1277
00:56:01,358 --> 00:56:03,325
May we ask
what you're doing here?

1278
00:56:03,360 --> 00:56:05,534
Well, someone has to do
the thinking for you.

1279
00:56:05,569 --> 00:56:07,225
- What?
- As your commander,
I thought it wise

1280
00:56:07,260 --> 00:56:08,882
to spy out the countryside.

1281
00:56:08,917 --> 00:56:11,609
The upper floor of this mill
was the only vantage point.

1282
00:56:11,644 --> 00:56:15,406
The last time
you spied out land,
sir Anthony died.

1283
00:56:15,441 --> 00:56:17,995
- Can you tell it in French?
- You can always turn back,
sir Dennis.

1284
00:56:18,029 --> 00:56:20,273
You want to hear my answer
to that?

1285
00:56:20,307 --> 00:56:21,757
As you will.

1286
00:56:21,792 --> 00:56:24,035
Shall we wake the others
and ride on?

1287
00:56:25,761 --> 00:56:29,282
[Joel]
Can't argue with this guy.
He's real smart.

1288
00:56:29,316 --> 00:56:30,766
- Gentlemen.
- Ladies.

1289
00:56:30,801 --> 00:56:33,424
[Tom] Age before beauty,
pearls before swine.

1290
00:56:36,358 --> 00:56:38,567
I know.
Don't get me started.

1291
00:56:40,258 --> 00:56:43,192
- Okay, pan right. Pan right.
- Pan. Keep panning.

1292
00:56:43,227 --> 00:56:44,677
Pan, pan, pan.

1293
00:56:44,711 --> 00:56:45,850
- Pan pizza.
- Keep panning.

1294
00:56:45,885 --> 00:56:47,058
Personal pan.

1295
00:56:47,093 --> 00:56:49,267
- [Lodac shouts]
- [Joel, robots gasp]

1296
00:56:49,302 --> 00:56:51,960
I almost killed him.
I tried.

1297
00:56:51,994 --> 00:56:53,893
I couldn't kill them both.

1298
00:56:53,927 --> 00:56:56,447
- That shield. I couldn't...
- Ehh!

1299
00:56:56,482 --> 00:56:59,070
- You look like worf.
- Shield?

1300
00:56:59,105 --> 00:57:00,831
Yes. That shield.

1301
00:57:00,865 --> 00:57:03,212
- Mark shields, Brooke shields,
shields and yarnell.
- Magic.

1302
00:57:03,247 --> 00:57:05,767
- Of course the boy has magic.
- [Hag] Sir George?

1303
00:57:05,801 --> 00:57:08,666
How else could he have
escaped three curses?

1304
00:57:08,701 --> 00:57:10,599
- Magic.
- [Tom] Courage!

1305
00:57:10,634 --> 00:57:12,290
I didn't even see it.

1306
00:57:12,325 --> 00:57:15,432
That foster mother, Sybil,
is working against me.

1307
00:57:15,466 --> 00:57:19,021
Her magic is strong, lodac.
I know it.

1308
00:57:19,056 --> 00:57:20,989
How will you counter it?

1309
00:57:21,023 --> 00:57:24,510
- Won't you need your ring?
- Not for Sybil's magic.

1310
00:57:24,544 --> 00:57:26,926
- [Joel] I can't look at you.
- But once I get my ring--

1311
00:57:26,960 --> 00:57:28,962
- I can do most anything.
- ...That's the end...

1312
00:57:28,997 --> 00:57:31,482
And the Princess will be fed
to my dragon.

1313
00:57:31,517 --> 00:57:34,209
- All I have to do now
is to redouble my magic.
- No spring?

1314
00:57:34,243 --> 00:57:37,039
- Bobblehead.
- And all you had to do
was destroy that frenchman.

1315
00:57:37,074 --> 00:57:38,524
You were too slow.

1316
00:57:38,558 --> 00:57:40,491
- You need to be taught
a lesson.
- No. No!

1317
00:57:40,526 --> 00:57:42,666
- Crawl, hag! Crawl!
- No! No!

1318
00:57:42,700 --> 00:57:44,530
For five, six, ten hours!

1319
00:57:44,564 --> 00:57:46,532
- Crawl until I need you again!
- [Hag] Lodac!

1320
00:57:46,566 --> 00:57:48,637
- Ooh.
- [High-pitched voice]
Help me! Help me!

1321
00:57:48,672 --> 00:57:51,709
[Joel]
She's finally the size two
she always wanted to be.

1322
00:57:51,744 --> 00:57:53,366
[Tom]
I guess so.

1323
00:57:53,400 --> 00:57:55,472
- Greetings, Sybil.
- [Gasps]

1324
00:57:55,506 --> 00:57:57,128
Lodac.

1325
00:57:57,163 --> 00:58:00,235
- [Lodac] You're looking older.
- Thank you, Charley's aunt.

1326
00:58:00,269 --> 00:58:02,927
We haven't met in what,
uh, 100 years?

1327
00:58:02,962 --> 00:58:04,860
I don't want to talk to you.

1328
00:58:04,895 --> 00:58:06,793
- But I want to talk to you.
- [Tom] About amway.

1329
00:58:06,828 --> 00:58:08,450
You've caused me
quite a lot of trouble.

1330
00:58:08,485 --> 00:58:10,521
Lodac, if you touch a hair

1331
00:58:10,556 --> 00:58:12,489
of my boy's head,
I'll fight you.

1332
00:58:12,523 --> 00:58:15,181
- [Joel] Weird sexual tension.
- Not a chance.

1333
00:58:15,215 --> 00:58:17,494
You've never had any real talent
for witchcraft.

1334
00:58:17,528 --> 00:58:19,634
- You're a hack!
- You were always tenth-rate.

1335
00:58:19,668 --> 00:58:20,911
"Tenth-rate"?

1336
00:58:20,945 --> 00:58:22,913
You're quite helpless, Sybil.

1337
00:58:22,947 --> 00:58:27,434
In four days,
the Princess helene will be fed
to my dragon,

1338
00:58:27,469 --> 00:58:29,436
and your George will die
even sooner than that.

1339
00:58:29,471 --> 00:58:31,887
There's nothing you can do

1340
00:58:31,922 --> 00:58:35,546
except look in your
stupid mirror or magic pool.

1341
00:58:35,581 --> 00:58:38,307
And now I'll
take care of that.

1342
00:58:38,342 --> 00:58:40,033
[Static]

1343
00:58:40,068 --> 00:58:41,966
Lodac, come back!
Come back!

1344
00:58:42,001 --> 00:58:44,969
Oh, now she'll have
to hang around all day Tuesday
waiting for the cable guy.

1345
00:58:45,004 --> 00:58:47,593
- Mm-hmm.
- Mirror of magic,
bring me a vision of lodac.

1346
00:58:47,627 --> 00:58:49,940
No!

1347
00:58:49,974 --> 00:58:51,458
[Tom]
Hmm.

1348
00:58:51,493 --> 00:58:53,115
Well, um,

1349
00:58:53,150 --> 00:58:55,152
guess I'll just have to
read a good book then.

1350
00:58:55,186 --> 00:58:58,465
Let's see.
Jurassic park, the firm...

1351
00:59:00,053 --> 00:59:02,366
- [Joel] Red room, red room.
- [Tom laughs]

1352
00:59:02,400 --> 00:59:04,610
Tenth-rate, am I?

1353
00:59:06,404 --> 00:59:08,234
Helpless, am I?

1354
00:59:08,268 --> 00:59:10,443
[Tom] It's estelle winwood's
one-woman show.

1355
00:59:10,477 --> 00:59:12,859
- [Joel] Give 'em hell, estelle.
- [Tom laughs]

1356
00:59:12,894 --> 00:59:14,481
I'd go see that.

1357
00:59:14,516 --> 00:59:17,243
- [Coughing]
- No, I can't do anything
to help George.

1358
00:59:17,277 --> 00:59:19,003
[Joel]
I'm going to get loaded.

1359
00:59:19,038 --> 00:59:22,006
- Hey, knock it off.
- Oh, lodac, lodac.
You'll be sorry for this.

1360
00:59:22,041 --> 00:59:24,837
[Joel]
Oh, there's an early
krupps machine right there.

1361
00:59:24,871 --> 00:59:26,493
Dear me.

1362
00:59:26,528 --> 00:59:28,806
It's a long time
since I've tried this one.

1363
00:59:28,841 --> 00:59:32,189
- ♪ Been a long time
since I rock and rolled ♪
- [indistinct]... or two.

1364
00:59:32,223 --> 00:59:36,469
One I'm sure.
Where is the unicorn powder?

1365
00:59:36,503 --> 00:59:38,333
Ahhh!

1366
00:59:38,367 --> 00:59:41,681
[All mimicking]
Ahhhhh!

1367
00:59:41,716 --> 00:59:44,339
- She should not be left alone.
- [Joel] Mm-mm.

1368
00:59:44,373 --> 00:59:46,306
[Tom] The type who writes
to dear Abby, you know.

1369
00:59:51,967 --> 00:59:54,763
Hey, keep it down in there!
People work in the morning!

1370
00:59:54,798 --> 00:59:56,765
I wonder what Sybil's
cooking up.

1371
00:59:56,800 --> 00:59:59,043
[Joel]
A lot of trouble.
[Laughs]

1372
00:59:59,078 --> 01:00:02,046
- She done it again!
- [Laughter]

1373
01:00:04,670 --> 01:00:08,052
♪♪ [All humming, whistling]

1374
01:00:08,087 --> 01:00:10,054
[Tom] Lucy, have you
been scheming again?

1375
01:00:10,089 --> 01:00:11,711
What have you done, Sybil?

1376
01:00:11,746 --> 01:00:13,402
Ahhh!

1377
01:00:13,437 --> 01:00:15,473
I've finished poor George.
That's what I've done.

1378
01:00:15,508 --> 01:00:18,131
I've taken his magic
away from him completely.

1379
01:00:18,166 --> 01:00:19,443
Ooh.

1380
01:00:20,617 --> 01:00:22,791
George has no more magic.

1381
01:00:22,826 --> 01:00:25,207
[Tom]
Wah, wah, wah.

1382
01:00:25,242 --> 01:00:27,727
[Joel]
We'll be back
with two separate views

1383
01:00:27,762 --> 01:00:30,696
of love, American style.

1384
01:00:31,869 --> 01:00:33,215
[Chuckles]

1385
01:00:33,250 --> 01:00:35,701
[Mutters]

1386
01:00:35,735 --> 01:00:37,875
Butch and Sundance:
The really early years.

1387
01:00:37,910 --> 01:00:40,395
Dennis! James!

1388
01:00:40,429 --> 01:00:42,708
James! Dennis!

1389
01:00:42,742 --> 01:00:44,710
Where are ya now?

1390
01:00:44,744 --> 01:00:46,332
There are the tracks
of their horses.

1391
01:00:46,366 --> 01:00:48,092
- There are the tracks
of my tears.
- I can't believe

1392
01:00:48,127 --> 01:00:49,818
- ...they're ahead of US.
- I can't believe
it's not butter.

1393
01:00:49,853 --> 01:00:52,200
I can't take this heat.
It's getting hotter
by the moment.

1394
01:00:52,234 --> 01:00:53,753
Why don't you take off
six or seven layers?

1395
01:00:53,788 --> 01:00:55,755
There's no way to let them
go on without US.

1396
01:00:55,790 --> 01:00:58,033
What could I do?
They were up before daylight

1397
01:00:58,068 --> 01:00:59,897
and belligerent, at that.

1398
01:00:59,932 --> 01:01:03,314
Sir Dennis said
that he couldn't trust me
to spy out the land any longer.

1399
01:01:03,349 --> 01:01:05,316
- You could've wakened US.
- [Tom] "Woken."

1400
01:01:05,351 --> 01:01:06,939
They swore they'd be back
before daylight.

1401
01:01:06,973 --> 01:01:08,941
- Dennis!
- James!

1402
01:01:08,975 --> 01:01:10,701
- Stella!
- By the looks up ahead,

1403
01:01:10,736 --> 01:01:12,979
they'll be burnt to a crisp
by this time.

1404
01:01:13,014 --> 01:01:15,810
By this time,
my lungs were burnt to a crisp.

1405
01:01:17,674 --> 01:01:21,298
Drizzil, drazzil,
drozzil, drone,

1406
01:01:21,332 --> 01:01:23,783
time for these two
to come home.

1407
01:01:23,818 --> 01:01:25,716
[Tom] Help, Mr. Willard!
Help!

1408
01:01:25,751 --> 01:01:28,477
[Joel] Hey, it's Peter Garrett
from midnight oil

1409
01:01:28,512 --> 01:01:30,928
and his double.

1410
01:01:30,963 --> 01:01:32,930
- Don't come! Go back!
- Ohh!

1411
01:01:32,965 --> 01:01:35,277
- Looks like their
jelly donuts blew up.
- Yeah.

1412
01:01:35,312 --> 01:01:37,521
- Go back! It's death up here!
- Ehh.

1413
01:01:37,555 --> 01:01:39,178
Go back!

1414
01:01:39,212 --> 01:01:41,525
[Tom]
To the five and dime,
Jimmy Dean, Jimmy Dean!

1415
01:01:41,559 --> 01:01:44,321
- [Joel] The hills have eyes.
- [Knights shouting]

1416
01:01:44,355 --> 01:01:45,805
[Joel]
Kind of.

1417
01:01:45,840 --> 01:01:47,117
[Knight]
Stay back!

1418
01:01:47,151 --> 01:01:49,740
- Huh.
- Uh--

1419
01:01:49,775 --> 01:01:52,018
huh?

1420
01:01:52,053 --> 01:01:54,607
- Dennis!
- James!

1421
01:01:54,641 --> 01:01:57,679
- [Tom] Elaine!
- [Shouting]

1422
01:02:00,924 --> 01:02:03,098
George, will you be looking
at that, man?

1423
01:02:03,133 --> 01:02:05,652
- I'm Irish, you know.
- It's not even hot,
and we're burning. Why?

1424
01:02:05,687 --> 01:02:07,724
- Aye, I'm Irish.
- Yes. Why, branton?

1425
01:02:07,758 --> 01:02:10,036
That, gentlemen,
is my secret.

1426
01:02:10,071 --> 01:02:13,005
Strong enough for a man
but made for a woman.

1427
01:02:13,039 --> 01:02:14,109
Huh?

1428
01:02:14,144 --> 01:02:15,904
[Clicks tongue]

1429
01:02:15,939 --> 01:02:17,837
- [Tom]
Wait! What's your secret?
- [Joel] Hey.

1430
01:02:17,872 --> 01:02:19,287
[Tom]
Come on! Tell me! Wait!

1431
01:02:19,321 --> 01:02:21,772
I can't hear you!
I'm not listening!

1432
01:02:23,843 --> 01:02:25,534
- Come on! You gotta tell US!
- Hey.

1433
01:02:25,569 --> 01:02:27,674
What's your secret?
Come on! Hey!

1434
01:02:27,709 --> 01:02:30,332
- I can't hear you!
[Gibberish]
- Hey!

1435
01:02:30,367 --> 01:02:32,610
Blah, blah, blah, blah.

1436
01:02:32,645 --> 01:02:34,405
[Imitating alarm ringing]

1437
01:02:34,440 --> 01:02:35,890
[Imitating train horn blaring]

1438
01:02:35,924 --> 01:02:37,684
[Imitating
train engine chugging]

1439
01:02:37,719 --> 01:02:39,100
[Horse whinnies]

1440
01:02:39,134 --> 01:02:42,103
[Imitating mister ed]
We're not going
in there, buddy.

1441
01:02:42,137 --> 01:02:44,174
[Whinnying]

1442
01:02:46,935 --> 01:02:48,765
The horses won't go
in the cave.

1443
01:02:48,799 --> 01:02:50,767
[Imitating mister ed]
Oh, I'm not going there.

1444
01:02:50,801 --> 01:02:53,148
It's not part
of the union contract.

1445
01:02:53,183 --> 01:02:54,771
[Sputters]

1446
01:02:57,187 --> 01:02:58,982
[Tom]
Park it here.

1447
01:02:59,016 --> 01:03:02,192
Lock it up.
There we go. Okay.

1448
01:03:03,883 --> 01:03:05,057
[All]
Surprise!

1449
01:03:05,091 --> 01:03:06,990
- ♪ Happy birthday to you
- branton!

1450
01:03:09,095 --> 01:03:10,579
Branton!

1451
01:03:10,614 --> 01:03:12,029
[Joel]
Echo.

1452
01:03:12,064 --> 01:03:14,100
- [Tom] Echo.
- [Joel] Guys, this isn't funny.

1453
01:03:14,135 --> 01:03:15,999
[Tom]
Funny, funny, funny.

1454
01:03:19,485 --> 01:03:21,452
- Where the "divil" is he?
- "Divil"?

1455
01:03:21,487 --> 01:03:23,316
- [Joel] He's Irish.
- Must be in here somewhere.

1456
01:03:23,351 --> 01:03:25,180
- [Lodoc]
You mean I was in here, George.
- Ooh.

1457
01:03:25,215 --> 01:03:27,113
- Branton.
- Huh?

1458
01:03:27,148 --> 01:03:29,288
I'm sorry
I have to leave you now.

1459
01:03:29,322 --> 01:03:32,463
A longstanding appointment
with the Princess helene

1460
01:03:32,498 --> 01:03:34,983
and also with my partner,
lodac.

1461
01:03:35,018 --> 01:03:36,674
- [Shouts]
- [Tom mutters]

1462
01:03:36,709 --> 01:03:38,987
Yes, his castle
is just over the hill.

1463
01:03:39,022 --> 01:03:41,162
Any, uh, last words
for either of them?

1464
01:03:41,196 --> 01:03:43,854
- [All] Help!
- You won't escape US, branton!

1465
01:03:43,889 --> 01:03:47,375
I already have escaped you,
George.

1466
01:03:47,409 --> 01:03:50,757
- [Explosion]
- [Joel] Ladies and gentlemen,
the magic of David Copperfield.

1467
01:03:50,792 --> 01:03:52,967
- Wow.
- [Tom] It's a brick.

1468
01:03:53,001 --> 01:03:54,347
[All]
♪ house

1469
01:03:54,382 --> 01:03:55,624
♪ ow ow

1470
01:03:55,659 --> 01:03:57,626
okay, where's Allen funt?

1471
01:03:57,661 --> 01:04:00,319
- [Joel] We thought
it would be funny.
- What do we do now?

1472
01:04:00,353 --> 01:04:03,218
We break out.
With ascalon's help.

1473
01:04:03,253 --> 01:04:05,220
- "Ascalon"?
- You ascalon. I'm bitter.

1474
01:04:05,255 --> 01:04:06,981
- My sword is magic.
- [Joel] ♪ ascalon

1475
01:04:07,015 --> 01:04:11,571
- ♪♪ [humming]
- It opens floors,
walls, gates, anything.

1476
01:04:11,606 --> 01:04:13,746
[Tom chuckles]

1477
01:04:13,780 --> 01:04:16,714
[Tom, indistinct]

1478
01:04:16,749 --> 01:04:18,578
- Lithium.
- It was magic.

1479
01:04:18,613 --> 01:04:20,028
Like your horse.

1480
01:04:20,063 --> 01:04:24,550
Perhaps lodac's magic
is a bit stronger.

1481
01:04:24,584 --> 01:04:26,932
[Joel]
Please wear safety goggles
if you're going to do that.

1482
01:04:26,966 --> 01:04:29,244
Aw, it's no use.
You'll only be dulling
the blade.

1483
01:04:29,279 --> 01:04:30,901
[Tom]
The movie's duller
than that blade.

1484
01:04:30,936 --> 01:04:32,938
Must be some other way
out, Patrick.

1485
01:04:35,561 --> 01:04:37,528
Well, I can only conclude
one thing:

1486
01:04:37,563 --> 01:04:40,911
- We're gonna die!
- [Tom, Joel exclaiming]

1487
01:04:40,946 --> 01:04:42,464
[Joel] You know,
I was just thinking.

1488
01:04:42,499 --> 01:04:43,949
A little wallpaper,
some carpeting--

1489
01:04:43,983 --> 01:04:45,916
this place could be fixed up
real nice.

1490
01:04:45,951 --> 01:04:47,297
Hmm.

1491
01:04:47,331 --> 01:04:51,301
[Tom]
I dub you sir moron.
Come on, dummy.

1492
01:04:51,335 --> 01:04:53,579
[Joel]
You know, how come all movies
end in a cave?

1493
01:04:53,613 --> 01:04:55,892
- To kill a mockingbirddidn't.
- Oh, you're right about that.

1494
01:04:55,926 --> 01:04:57,686
- At long last lovedidn't.
- Oh, that's two.

1495
01:04:57,721 --> 01:04:59,240
To have and have not,
Anna christie.

1496
01:04:59,274 --> 01:05:01,725
[Joel]
Three-- you're right.
Just about all of them.

1497
01:05:01,759 --> 01:05:03,209
None of them ended in a cave.

1498
01:05:03,244 --> 01:05:05,798
This place is filled
with bread sticks!

1499
01:05:05,832 --> 01:05:08,007
Mmm.

1500
01:05:08,042 --> 01:05:10,907
[Tom] Hey, are we in
someone's epiglottis?

1501
01:05:13,771 --> 01:05:16,395
Oh, just a cheap Bert I. Gordon
special effect.

1502
01:05:16,429 --> 01:05:18,121
[Tom]
Yep. Yep.

1503
01:05:20,019 --> 01:05:22,366
No, nothing happening
with the plot over here.

1504
01:05:22,401 --> 01:05:23,367
[Tom, Joel]
Mmm.

1505
01:05:23,402 --> 01:05:25,714
[Tom]
Nothing over here either.

1506
01:05:25,749 --> 01:05:27,371
All clear.

1507
01:05:27,406 --> 01:05:28,821
It's...

1508
01:05:28,855 --> 01:05:30,271
Hmm?

1509
01:05:31,410 --> 01:05:34,033
[Joel]
It's ho chi minh.

1510
01:05:36,346 --> 01:05:38,175
Patrick!

1511
01:05:38,210 --> 01:05:39,970
It's the new monkeys!

1512
01:05:42,490 --> 01:05:45,182
[Joel, robots,
in ghostly voices]
Guilty. Guilty.

1513
01:05:45,217 --> 01:05:47,805
Guilty. Guilty.

1514
01:05:47,840 --> 01:05:50,774
[Joel] You know what? I just
realized we're in the ghost
and Mr. Chicken!

1515
01:05:50,808 --> 01:05:53,259
[Tom]
Let's get outta here!

1516
01:05:53,294 --> 01:05:54,674
Run!

1517
01:05:54,709 --> 01:05:56,159
- [Ghostly voices screaming]
- Oh, they're not dead.

1518
01:05:56,193 --> 01:05:58,092
They're just
metaphysically challenged.

1519
01:05:58,126 --> 01:05:59,886
- Oh.
- Yeah.

1520
01:06:02,130 --> 01:06:04,753
♪♪ [Tom humming eerily]

1521
01:06:04,788 --> 01:06:07,170
The faces of those he's wronged
are floating up at him!

1522
01:06:07,204 --> 01:06:08,757
[Screaming continues]

1523
01:06:08,792 --> 01:06:09,896
[Tom]
Jimmy smits.

1524
01:06:15,316 --> 01:06:18,560
[Joel]
Halls vapor action! Yeah!

1525
01:06:23,634 --> 01:06:24,601
[Tom]
So?

1526
01:06:24,635 --> 01:06:26,672
I am the god of hellfire,

1527
01:06:26,706 --> 01:06:28,743
and I brim you!

1528
01:06:29,986 --> 01:06:32,712
Ah, he's battling
his own demons here.

1529
01:06:32,747 --> 01:06:36,130
[Joel]
Oh, boy. He's becoming
the green ghost game.

1530
01:06:36,164 --> 01:06:37,959
[Tom]
Neat.

1531
01:06:37,994 --> 01:06:40,237
There's something
you don't see every day.

1532
01:06:40,272 --> 01:06:42,239
- Pretty much.
Guy writhing in hell.
- Help me! Help me!

1533
01:06:42,274 --> 01:06:44,586
Soul torn out
from underneath him.

1534
01:06:44,621 --> 01:06:47,486
- [Patrick] Through the wall!
- [Tom] Ugh!

1535
01:06:47,520 --> 01:06:49,419
So, uh,
I'll just leave, then,

1536
01:06:49,453 --> 01:06:50,972
I guess.

1537
01:06:53,837 --> 01:06:58,117
Ah, great.
Now we're stuck in here.

1538
01:06:58,152 --> 01:07:00,602
[Tom]
That was an interesting scene,
but one question:

1539
01:07:00,637 --> 01:07:02,328
What the heck
just happened here?

1540
01:07:02,363 --> 01:07:03,709
[Joel]
Trippy.

1541
01:07:03,743 --> 01:07:06,919
[Strained]
I'll be here if you need me,
George.

1542
01:07:06,953 --> 01:07:10,371
[Tom]
He's dead, Jim.

1543
01:07:10,405 --> 01:07:12,511
[Joel]
See? You get to pick out
your own steak.

1544
01:07:12,545 --> 01:07:14,547
Watch this.
I'll have that one.

1545
01:07:14,582 --> 01:07:16,411
Huh. Neat stuff,
but is it art?

1546
01:07:16,446 --> 01:07:18,344
He's escaped again!

1547
01:07:18,379 --> 01:07:20,208
- Sir Patrick too?
- And Toto too.

1548
01:07:20,243 --> 01:07:22,728
No. Patrick is finished.

1549
01:07:22,762 --> 01:07:25,489
But sir George
is riding this way.

1550
01:07:25,524 --> 01:07:28,285
There was no way
out of that cave, lodac.

1551
01:07:28,320 --> 01:07:30,563
- Was it magic?
- [Tom] Or was it memorex?

1552
01:07:30,598 --> 01:07:32,255
- You decide.
- Not magic.

1553
01:07:32,289 --> 01:07:33,946
Then how did George escape?

1554
01:07:33,980 --> 01:07:36,811
- I think-- yes.
- [Crow sighs]

1555
01:07:36,845 --> 01:07:39,365
- Something stronger than magic.
- [Joel] Double secret magic.

1556
01:07:39,400 --> 01:07:41,333
A power of Patrick's faith.

1557
01:07:50,997 --> 01:07:54,104
Fellas, I've got
something to say, and there's
only one way to do it,

1558
01:07:54,139 --> 01:07:55,795
so give me center stage!

1559
01:07:55,830 --> 01:07:57,487
- Hey, be my guest.
It's all yours.
- Thank you.

1560
01:07:57,521 --> 01:07:58,660
- [Clears throat]
- ♪♪ [piano]

1561
01:07:58,695 --> 01:08:02,630
♪ As you guys
I'm sure remember ♪

1562
01:08:02,664 --> 01:08:07,807
♪ I think it was maybe
last December ♪

1563
01:08:07,842 --> 01:08:11,190
♪ I fell pell-mell

1564
01:08:11,225 --> 01:08:13,675
♪ for Kim cattrall

1565
01:08:13,710 --> 01:08:16,368
[scoffs]
Yeah, we remember.
Please don't remind US.

1566
01:08:16,402 --> 01:08:17,714
- Ooh!
- Ah.

1567
01:08:17,748 --> 01:08:19,750
♪ But now I'm over that

1568
01:08:19,785 --> 01:08:20,924
well, that's good.

1569
01:08:20,958 --> 01:08:23,478
♪ We had a little spat

1570
01:08:23,513 --> 01:08:24,997
[scoffs]
In your dreams, buddy.

1571
01:08:25,031 --> 01:08:26,792
♪ I'm older, wiser

1572
01:08:26,826 --> 01:08:29,553
♪ and I know
that my true love ♪

1573
01:08:29,588 --> 01:08:32,315
♪ is really named

1574
01:08:33,695 --> 01:08:35,145
♪ estelle

1575
01:08:35,180 --> 01:08:37,009
- [Tom] Winwood?
- [Crow] She's swell!

1576
01:08:37,043 --> 01:08:38,321
[Tom laughs]

1577
01:08:38,355 --> 01:08:39,977
♪ She's cute

1578
01:08:40,012 --> 01:08:41,979
♪ she's rooty-toot-toot

1579
01:08:42,014 --> 01:08:44,534
♪ I bet she smells
like juicy fruit ♪

1580
01:08:44,568 --> 01:08:47,675
- Ehh!
- ♪ she can really
play a witch ♪

1581
01:08:47,709 --> 01:08:51,506
- Ridiculous.
- ♪ she was even on bewitched ♪

1582
01:08:51,541 --> 01:08:55,061
♪ and I'm bewildered
and bothered ♪

1583
01:08:55,096 --> 01:08:57,167
Oh, but, crow,
hold on a second.
Hold on.

1584
01:08:57,202 --> 01:08:58,513
Thank you.
[Scoffs]

1585
01:08:58,548 --> 01:09:00,032
♪♪ [Piano]

1586
01:09:00,066 --> 01:09:03,242
♪ Crow, you are my buddy

1587
01:09:03,277 --> 01:09:06,935
♪ and maybe I'm a fuddy-duddy

1588
01:09:06,970 --> 01:09:08,868
♪ but step back, please

1589
01:09:08,903 --> 01:09:12,596
♪ and think about some stuff

1590
01:09:12,631 --> 01:09:14,253
like what?

1591
01:09:14,288 --> 01:09:17,498
♪ I'm sure that she seems nice

1592
01:09:17,532 --> 01:09:19,224
- oh, yes.
- ♪ but I bet

1593
01:09:19,258 --> 01:09:21,778
♪ she's more
than twice your age ♪

1594
01:09:21,812 --> 01:09:25,506
- Well...
- ♪ and with your love
it might not matter ♪

1595
01:09:25,540 --> 01:09:28,543
♪ but can she
control her bladder ♪

1596
01:09:28,578 --> 01:09:30,131
- Shut up! Shut up! Gee!
- [Laughing]

1597
01:09:30,166 --> 01:09:31,960
- Come on.
- Okay, okay.

1598
01:09:31,995 --> 01:09:34,549
But, Joel, there's a whole
other set of issues here.

1599
01:09:34,584 --> 01:09:36,689
If I may, crow,
as a friend,

1600
01:09:36,724 --> 01:09:38,415
as a trusted advisor?

1601
01:09:38,450 --> 01:09:40,555
- Well...
- Okay.

1602
01:09:40,590 --> 01:09:42,212
♪ Here's a quick list
of people ♪

1603
01:09:42,247 --> 01:09:45,560
♪ much better-looking
than estelle ♪

1604
01:09:45,595 --> 01:09:48,080
- You're so shallow.
- ♪ Fred biletnikoff,
Brandon tartikoff ♪

1605
01:09:48,114 --> 01:09:50,151
- ♪ sid and Marty krofft
- very funny.

1606
01:09:50,186 --> 01:09:52,498
♪ Fred gwynne and Anthony Quinn
and rin tin tin ♪

1607
01:09:52,533 --> 01:09:55,260
♪ and Pearl Bailey
and moms mabley
and mayor daley ♪

1608
01:09:55,294 --> 01:09:57,883
- ♪ and Hank and Phoebe snow
- I'm not listening.

1609
01:09:57,917 --> 01:10:00,230
♪ Ethel merman
and pee wee Herman
and strom thurmond ♪

1610
01:10:00,265 --> 01:10:03,302
♪ and Vince lombardi
and George and Jesus Jones ♪

1611
01:10:03,337 --> 01:10:04,648
I don't care!
I don't care!

1612
01:10:04,683 --> 01:10:06,512
♪ Edith head and mister ed

1613
01:10:06,547 --> 01:10:10,102
♪ and nostradamus
and Danny Thomas
and sherman hemsley ♪

1614
01:10:10,136 --> 01:10:12,242
